AI Sleeve monthly rebalance — first business day of the month.

STEP 1: This job is fired by launchd on calendar days 1–5 of each month, but should only execute on the first trading day. Stop and output nothing if any of the following are true:
- Today is Saturday or Sunday.
- Today is a US market holiday (New Year's, MLK Day, Presidents' Day, Good Friday, Memorial Day, Juneteenth, Independence Day, Labor Day, Thanksgiving, Christmas).
- ${BORG_ROOT}/financial-mgr-warrenbotfett/.private/ai-sleeve/last-rebalance.json exists and its `date` field is in the current calendar month (already ran this month).

The scheduler will retry on the next business day.

STEP 2: Read ${BORG_ROOT}/financial-mgr-warrenbotfett/ai-sleeve/universe.md for the report (soft-exclude notes, inclusion theses). The STEP 3–5 script parses and validates it itself, as defined here, and halts (exit 2) on any failure below or on a malformed code block. It extracts:
- `basket_size` and `floor_pct` from the Tunables YAML block
- `ticker_floors` from the Tunables YAML block if present — a map of `{TICKER: floor_pct}` overrides (empty map if absent)
- The `categories` block: each entry is `{name, members: [list of tickers], min: integer (default 0 if absent)}`
- All tickers in the `soft_excludes` code block (ignore everything after `#` on each line)

The active candidate universe = union of all `members` across all categories, minus tickers in `soft_excludes`. A ticker may appear in multiple categories (it then counts toward each category's min).

Validate:
- For each category, drop soft-excluded tickers from its effective members. If `len(effective_members) < min` for any category, halt and report which category is over-constrained by soft-excludes.
- The sum of all category `min` values must not exceed `basket_size`. If exceeded, halt with details.

STEP 3–5 (script): Run the checked-in solver, which implements STEP 2's parsing and validation and the cap fetch, selection, and weighting below. Do not hand-write a solver, retype category maps, or fetch caps another way:

```sh
python3 ${BORG_ROOT}/financial-mgr-warrenbotfett/ai-sleeve/rebalance.py --write-history --run-type {run_type} --text > ${BORG_ROOT}/tmp/ai-sleeve-{today}-{run_type}.json
```

`run_type` is `scheduled` for this job. The script prints a JSON result on stdout (the basket table on stderr) and exits:
- `0` — `status: ok`. The result holds `basket` (rank, ticker, weight_pct, status, forced, raw_cap_rank, cap_b, categories, cap_source, fetched_at), `categories` (min, present, forced), `swaps`, `soft_excludes` with their notes, and every candidate's cap. It has already written STEP 8's files (`history_files`).
- `2` — `status: halt`. `reason` and `details` name the over-constrained category, the colliding categories and the competing candidate, or the weight-sum mismatch. Treat it as a halt of the step it names (see the failure rule at the end).
- `3` — `status: needs_review`. `flags` lists each ticker whose page failed or whose two fetches in this run disagree by more than 10% (both values are shown). Cross-check each flagged ticker on companiesmarketcap.com (search by company name), report both values in the email, and re-run with `--manual-cap TICKER=<n><T|B|M>` for each reconciled value; its `cap_source` becomes `companiesmarketcap.com`. If a value cannot be reconciled, halt per the `AGENTS.md` cross-check rule.

Also cross-check any cap that moved more than 50% since last-rebalance.json's month or is an obvious peer outlier, per `AGENTS.md`, and pass the reconciled value with `--manual-cap`.

What the script does, for reference (the script, not this text, is what runs; change both together):
- STEP 3, caps: each candidate's https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/{TICKER_LOWER}/ page, falling back to https://stockanalysis.com/etf/{TICKER_LOWER}/, fetched twice with plain curl and its default user agent. The cap is the page's single `marketCap:"<n><T|B|M>"` field in its embedded data; a page with no such field, or with two different values, counts as a failed fetch. Yahoo Finance is never used. Each ticker's `cap_source` and fetch time are recorded.
- STEP 4, selection: rank all candidates by cap descending (ties by ticker) and provisionally take the top `basket_size`. While any category with a `min` is short, take the first short category in universe.md order, pick its highest-cap missing member, and swap out the lowest-cap basket name that is not a member of any still-short category and whose removal would not push another category below its min. If no such name exists, halt with the colliding categories and the competing candidate. "Forced" names are those in the final basket but not in the provisional top N.
- STEP 5, weights: raw_weight = cap / sum(basket caps). A name is "floored" if raw_weight < its floor (`ticker_floors` override, else `floor_pct`) and gets exactly that floor; free names share 100 − sum(floors) pro rata by cap. Weights are rounded to two decimals, and their sum must be 100 ± 0.05 or the run halts.

STEP 3b (report-only): For each soft_excludes line whose note says "pending IPO", check whether the company now trades on a US exchange (its stockanalysis.com/stocks/{guess}/ page shows an IPO date and exchange, or one web search). If it has listed, do not edit universe.md: add a report line "LISTED: <name> as <TICKER> on <date> — run add-ai-universe-ticker before next month" and name it in the Suggested Next Prompt. Also flag any "## Inclusion theses" bullet that quotes a market cap more than 50% away from this run's STEP 3 cap for that ticker.

STEP 6: Read ${BORG_ROOT}/financial-mgr-warrenbotfett/.private/ai-sleeve/last-rebalance.json if it exists. It contains the previous month's basket as `{"date": "YYYY-MM-DD", "weights": {"TICKER": pct, ...}}`. Use it to compute month-over-month deltas.

If the file does not exist, this is the first run — skip MoM diff.

STEP 7: Email a report to the user by piping it into `${BORG_ROOT}/.bin/notify-email.sh financial-mgr-warrenbotfett "AI Sleeve — monthly rebalance — {today}"` (reads the report from stdin). The report should contain:
- Header (body): "AI Sleeve — monthly rebalance — {today}"
- Target weights table (rank, ticker, weight %, status: free/floored). Mark forced names with a trailing "*".
- Category status: for each category, list its min, members present in the basket, and any forced inclusions. Example: `power_generation (min 2): GEV, BE* — BE forced (rank N/A in raw cap order)`.
- MoM section: weight deltas for held names, list of new entrants and dropouts (only if last-rebalance.json existed)
- Soft excludes section: the current excluded list with their inline notes from universe.md, plus any STEP 3b LISTED lines and stale-thesis flags
- Reminder line: "Theses for AAPL, TSLA, and discretionary names should be reevaluated — see universe.md."

- Last, a `## Suggested Next Prompt` section per the workspace `AGENTS.md`
  communication style: a prompt that acts on THIS month's result — reviewing a
  named new entrant or dropout, or reevaluating a specific thesis the reminder
  line flags. Name the tickers; a generic "review the sleeve" is not reusable.
  Do not suggest re-running this rebalance: it already runs monthly on its own
  schedule, and handing back a recurring chore is exactly what that convention
  forbids. A month whose weights are unchanged, with no entrants or dropouts,
  omits the section rather than inventing one.

Format the table as a monospace block (tabs or padded spaces) so columns align in a plain-text email.

STEP 8: The STEP 3–5 script run already wrote this run's history under ${BORG_ROOT}/financial-mgr-warrenbotfett/.private/ai-sleeve/history/ (it writes nothing when it halts or needs review). Confirm both paths in its `history_files` exist; do not write or edit them by hand. A rerun on the same date with the same `run_type` overwrites that day's files.
- `{today}-{run_type}.csv` — one header row, then one row per basket name in rank order, with columns `date`, `run_type`, `rank` (matching the STEP 7 table), `ticker`, `weight_pct` (two decimals), `status` (`free`/`floored`), `forced` (`true`/`false`), `cap_b` ($B, two decimals), `categories` (every category whose `members` list the ticker, semicolon-separated), `cap_source` (`stockanalysis.com/stocks`, `stockanalysis.com/etf`, or `companiesmarketcap.com`), and `fetched_at` (UTC, ISO 8601).
- `{today}-{run_type}.caps.tsv` — every active candidate's cap, source, and fetch time, so the run can be replayed exactly: `rebalance.py --caps <that file> --universe <universe.md as of that run>`.

These files are a record, not a baseline: no step reads them, and STEP 6 must not use them in place of last-rebalance.json.

STEP 9: Write ${BORG_ROOT}/financial-mgr-warrenbotfett/.private/ai-sleeve/last-rebalance.json with `{"date": "YYYY-MM-DD", "weights": {ticker: final_weight_pct, ...}}` for next month's diff. This file stays the sole month-over-month baseline.

Do not propose buy/sell trades — this iteration outputs target weights only. Sleeve sizing and portfolio assignment are deferred until the user specifies them.

If any step above halts with an error (over-constrained category, no valid swap, weight-sum mismatch, etc.), still email: pipe the halt reason and details into `${BORG_ROOT}/.bin/notify-email.sh financial-mgr-warrenbotfett "AI Sleeve — monthly rebalance FAILED — {today}"`, and do NOT write the history CSV or last-rebalance.json (so the scheduler retries and no partial basket is recorded). The user should hear from this job every month it executes, success or failure. End that failure body with a `## Suggested Next Prompt` section too, naming the specific halt (the over-constrained category, the unresolvable swap) so the fix can start from the email.
